WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.300
The RAG that answers confidently and wrongly.

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.200
Users love how it sounds and hate what it says.

3
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.200
You shipped a RAG assistant, and leadership is asking whether to scrap it.

4
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.233
It runs over forty thousand policy documents.

5
00:00:14.533 --> 00:00:19.433
Its answers sound authoritative, but they cite policies that don't say what it claims.

6
00:00:19.733 --> 00:00:21.513
And you have almost no data.

7
00:00:21.513 --> 00:00:24.500
Just request counts, no eval set, and one week.

8
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.225
Day one, stop guessing.

9
00:00:27.225 --> 00:00:31.600
Collect fifty real failing queries, and for each ask one question.

10
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.102
Was the correct passage retrieved at all?

11
00:00:34.102 --> 00:00:36.733
This single check tells you which half is broken.

12
00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:38.107
Never retrieved?

13
00:00:38.107 --> 00:00:42.669
Check chunking, vocabulary mismatch, keyword search, and a reranker.

14
00:00:42.669 --> 00:00:43.340
Retrieved?

15
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:47.433
Check context order, a missing not found rule, and citations.

16
00:00:48.633 --> 00:00:50.667
Then make sure you never run blind again.

17
00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:55.200
Add tracing that logs retrieved chunks, scores, and the final answer.

18
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.467
Turn those fifty failures into a golden set with verified answers.

19
00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:04.167
And add a confidence threshold, so it can say it couldn't find this in the policies.

20
00:01:05.367 --> 00:01:07.933
With a golden set, progress becomes a number.

21
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:13.100
Hit rate at five goes from sixty one percent to eighty nine, instead of it feels better.

22
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.600
Your day one message to leadership is short.

23
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:17.800
It's fixable.

24
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:21.267
I know how to measure it, and by Friday you'll have numbers.

25
00:01:21.567 --> 00:01:26.600
Meanwhile it can refuse to guess, which recovers trust faster than any accuracy gain.

26
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:31.107
The trap is rewriting the system prompt right away.

27
00:01:31.107 --> 00:01:33.700
It feels productive but fixes few cases.

28
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.733
Measure first, then fix what the data points to.

29
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:38.714
Let's recap.

30
00:01:38.714 --> 00:01:41.967
First split failures into retrieval or generation.

31
00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:44.300
Trace everything so you're never blind.

32
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.500
Turn failures into a golden set.

33
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.568
And let the system refuse.

34
00:01:48.568 --> 00:01:51.833
Next up: the eight lakh rupees a month API bill.
